WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.785
This is Draw.

2
00:00:01.835 --> 00:00:06.689
You'll build three things with blocks: a square, a hexagon, and a pattern.

3
00:00:07.239 --> 00:00:08.668
Run shows the path.

4
00:00:08.718 --> 00:00:11.343
Check scores the puzzle named on its button.

5
00:00:11.993 --> 00:00:14.574
First, the two blocks that steer the path.

6
00:00:15.024 --> 00:00:18.536
The path starts at the dot, and the turtle faces right.

7
00:00:18.886 --> 00:00:21.889
Move goes forward, the way the turtle faces.

8
00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:24.723
Move 100 draws one straight line.

9
00:00:25.073 --> 00:00:27.263
Turn changes the way it faces.

10
00:00:27.313 --> 00:00:31.612
Turn 90 is a quarter turn, to the left. It draws nothing.

11
00:00:31.962 --> 00:00:37.010
Now move 100 again, and the line goes up, because the turtle faces up.

12
00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:41.690
To build that, type a number, then press Add move, or Add turn.

13
00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:43.979
Each press adds a block at the end.

14
00:00:44.329 --> 00:00:47.989
For a square, you could add move, turn, four times over.

15
00:00:48.039 --> 00:00:49.537
That's eight blocks.

16
00:00:49.987 --> 00:00:51.782
Repeat saves the copying.

17
00:00:51.832 --> 00:00:55.950
Clear, then add one move 100, and one turn 90.

18
00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:58.395
Run it, and you get one side.

19
00:00:58.945 --> 00:01:02.554
Type 4 in the repeat box, and press Wrap in repeat.

20
00:01:02.604 --> 00:01:05.682
Everything so far goes inside a repeat 4.

21
00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:07.099
Run again.

22
00:01:07.149 --> 00:01:10.304
The same square, from three blocks instead of eight.

23
00:01:10.854 --> 00:01:14.400
Undo takes back the last block, or unwraps a wrap.

24
00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:16.290
Clear empties the program.

25
00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:20.757
That's the square task. Predict, then build, then Run.

26
00:01:20.807 --> 00:01:24.313
The path starts at the dot, and the first move leaves it.

27
00:01:24.663 --> 00:01:31.310
Four quarter turns make 360 degrees, one full turn, so the path closes at the dot.

28
00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.100
Then press Check the square.

29
00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:37.644
Next, a triangle demonstration, not your next puzzle.

30
00:01:37.694 --> 00:01:41.445
Repeat 3: move 100, turn 120.

31
00:01:41.795 --> 00:01:44.299
The three turns still make one full turn.

32
00:01:44.349 --> 00:01:48.672
360 divided by 3 is 120.

33
00:01:49.022 --> 00:01:51.963
The turn is the outside change of direction.

34
00:01:52.013 --> 00:01:54.512
The inside corner is a different number.

35
00:01:54.562 --> 00:01:57.236
Type the turn, not the inside corner.

36
00:01:57.986 --> 00:02:02.288
Hide this example before the hexagon. It's a different task.

37
00:02:02.838 --> 00:02:06.075
Your practice shape is a hexagon, with six sides.

38
00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:09.306
The turns in a closed shape add up to a full turn.

39
00:02:09.356 --> 00:02:11.455
Divide by the number of sides.

40
00:02:11.805 --> 00:02:18.201
The page can show a program that does not close: repeat 6, move 100, turn 90.

41
00:02:18.551 --> 00:02:21.605
Six turns of 90 are more than a full turn.

42
00:02:21.655 --> 00:02:25.329
The path runs over itself, and ends away from the dot.

43
00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:29.054
Now you try. What turn closes six sides?

44
00:02:29.104 --> 00:02:31.931
Build it, Run it, and check that it closes.

45
00:02:35.881 --> 00:02:38.608
Last, a pattern inspired by a rose.

46
00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:41.845
It's geometric, not a simulation of weaving.

47
00:02:42.195 --> 00:02:44.868
Vary the repeat, the move, or the turn.

48
00:02:44.918 --> 00:02:48.592
Predict what will change, Run, then say what you changed.

49
00:02:49.142 --> 00:02:53.556
Here's one start: move 40, turn 45, in a repeat 8.

50
00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:57.544
Eight turns of 45 make one full turn, so it closes.

51
00:02:57.894 --> 00:03:02.462
Change just the turn, to 135. Predict, then Run.

52
00:03:02.512 --> 00:03:03.853
Now it's a star.

53
00:03:03.903 --> 00:03:08.840
Eight turns of 135 go around three times, and still close.

54
00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:14.538
Or wrap twice: a square, then turn 30, all inside a repeat 12.

55
00:03:14.588 --> 00:03:16.473
That makes a ring of squares.

56
00:03:17.023 --> 00:03:19.158
It doesn't have to look like a flower.

57
00:03:19.508 --> 00:03:20.679
Now you try.

58
00:03:20.729 --> 00:03:23.713
Which number will you change, and what do you predict?

59
00:03:23.763 --> 00:03:27.181
Run it, write what changed, and press Check the pattern.

60
00:03:31.131 --> 00:03:34.919
Each puzzle keeps its own result. The blocks are the program.

61
00:03:34.969 --> 00:03:37.086
The drawing is the output.
